          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding qualification limits its degree but does not weaken the clear avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The occurrence invokes and affirms Cordelia's existing filial bond rather than creating a new promise. “According to my bond” makes the avowed love an enactment of what binds her as Lear's daughter, and her immediate elaboration identifies the associated duties of reciprocation, obedience, love, and honour. Thus the contextual material bears directly on this occurrence rather than constituting a separate nearby promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The expression is a measured, deliberately qualified answer in a public exchange, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly controls and limits what she says.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.89,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and invocation of filial obligation, the occurrence corrects the terms of Lear's competitive love test: Cordelia refuses hyperbolic comparison and defines her love as exactly proportionate to her bond. This restrained refusal of flattering excess is a moderate residual function.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to declare which loves him most while distributing their dowers. Cordelia answers after refusing to produce the expansive rhetoric used by her sisters. Her focal avowal is immediately qualified as love owed under her filial bond and is later explained through the duties of a daughter.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms an existing filial undertaking: to return the duties appropriate to Lear as her father through obedience, love, and honour. She does not make a new future-tense promise or undertake unlimited devotion. Lear does not accept this bounded affirmation as an adequate answer; he tells her to amend her speech and then repudiates their paternal relationship. His rejection concerns the sufficiency and presentation of her declaration, not the absence of undertaking force in her invocation of the filial bond.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognised as William Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification is based on the supplied passages rather than additional remembered plot facts.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene strongly supports sincere affection through Cordelia's asides and later insistence that her heart agrees with her words, but literary evidence cannot establish an actual mental state with certainty. The precise practical extent of the invoked filial duties is not fully specified, and Lear rejects rather than mutually ratifies Cordelia's formulation.\"}"
        }
